1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qa5 7. Qd2 Nc6 8. Nf3
Qa4 $2 ({He wants to stop a later a4 and Ba3, but this is premature; he should
play} 8... Nge7 {. Now he never gets another chance.}) 9. dxc5 $1 {The tripled
pawns look weak but they are not. Black cannot get at them and they cramp him
seriously.} Nge7 10. Bd3 O-O 11. O-O f6 {This creates a further weakness, but
he must relieve his congestion. White was threatening Qg5, followed by Qh5 and
Ng5.} 12. Re1 fxe5 13. Nxe5 Qh4 14. Nf3 Qh5 15. Qg5 $1 {Beautifully simple.}
Qf7 ({If} 15... Qxg5 {then} 16. Nxg5 {wins the e-pawn or h-pawn and with it
the game.}) 16. Be3 h6 ({If} 16... Bd7 {then} 17. Rab1 Rab8 18. Bd4 {and later
on Ne5 or Be5. Black is positionally lost.}) 17. Qg3 Nf5 18. Bxf5 exf5 19. Bf4
$1 ({Not} 19. Bxh6 $2 f4 20. Qg5 Kh8 $1) 19... Be6 $2 ({Losing at once but
what can he do?} 19... Bd7 20. Rab1 {[and now some moves that don't make sense]
20...Kt-K5 21 Q-K1 BxP}) ({or} 19... g5 20. h4 g4 21. Bxh6) (19... Kh8 {
is relatively best, but Black is too cramped to have any real chance of
survival.}) 20. Bxh6 $1 f4 21. Qxg7+ $1 Qxg7 22. Bxg7 {Sources: The Times, 31
December 1962; Sunday Times, 3 February 1963 (notes by Alexander); BCM, March
1963, p83; CHESS/28/424-425 January 1963, p114} (22. Bxg7 Kxg7 23. Rxe6 {
and Black has nothing to show for his three pawns.}) 1-0

