1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. b4 {An old move of Reti's, brought back into popularity
by Taimanov and Smyslov. It contains more sting than appears at first sight,
its basic objects being to reinforce the pressure on White's KB and to gain
space on the queenside.} Bg7 4. Bb2 O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O a5 7. b5 a4 $1 {
The only logical follow-up of Black's 6th move. The idea is two-fold, to
prevent White from backing up his a-pawn, and to make White's queenside
development awkward; e.g. 8 Nc3 a3!} 8. d4 c6 9. c4 cxb5 10. cxb5 Nbd7 11. Na3
Nb6 {The black knight is safe in front of b5} 12. Re1 Bf5 $2 ({This move is
too risky. I was trying to induce White to create weaknesses in his own
position but this allows him to develop a powerful pawn centre. Simplest was} 
12... d5 {when White has to justify his opening.}) 13. Nh4 $1 Be4 14. f3 Bd5
15. e4 Be6 16. Bf1 $2 ({In avoiding the complications of} 16. d5 g5 17. dxe6
gxh4 18. Bh3 fxe6 19. Bxe6+ Kh8 {, when he would have a good game, he wastes
time which Black uses profitably.}) 16... Rc8 17. Ng2 Bh3 18. Qd2 Nfd7 $1 19.
Rac1 e5 $1 20. Rxc8 Qxc8 21. Rc1 Qd8 22. Be2 d5 $3 {Not only logical but
powerful. Black clears the centre and takes advantage of White's awkwardly
placed pieces. It took me over half an hour to convince myself that the move
was tactically correct.} 23. exd5 ({My main variation was the most beautiful
one I have ever conceived:} 23. dxe5 dxe4 $1 24. f4 Nxe5 $3 25. Qxd8 Nf3+ 26.
Bxf3 Rxd8 27. Bxg7 exf3 28. Bf6 Rd2 $1 {and Black wins, however White
continues. This variation reveals how carelessly and uncharacteristically
Smyslov has played his last few moves.}) 23... Bxg2 $1 24. Kxg2 exd4 (24...
Nxd5 $2 {is no good} 25. dxe5 N5b6 26. f4 $18) 25. Nc2 $1 ({As} 25. Bxd4 Bxd4
26. Qxd4 Qe7 $1 27. Qd3 Ne5 {and 28...Nxd5 would give Black a tremendous
position.}) 25... Re8 26. Bxd4 Bxd4 27. Nxd4 Nxd5 28. Nc2 Nc5 $1 {Not only
threatening to win the queen by 29...Ne3+ but preventing 29 Bd3 for the same
reason.} 29. Rd1 Qf6 30. Bc4 Nb6 31. Bf1 Ne6 32. Qf2 h5 $1 {As White cannot
take the knight - 33 Qxb6?? Nf4+ - I create more weaknesses in White's
kingside.} 33. h4 Rc8 34. Nb4 Rd8 35. Rxd8+ Qxd8 36. a3 Nd5 {At this juncture
Smyslov surprised me by saying: ''Are you playing for a win?'' What could I
say? I still have the better of it, because of White's kingside weaknesses and
his awkward bishop, but my nerve failed me when I visualised trying to win
against Smyslov. Normally I should have played on but here, somewhat cowardly,
I agreed to a draw... Source: CHESS/28/424-425 January 1963, p113} 1/2-1/2

