{(Notes by Littlewood unless otherwise stated.)} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. g3 Bg7
4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O Nbd7 7. b3 c6 ({And not} 7... Ne4 8. Nxe4 $1 Bxa1
9. d4 {and 10 Bh6.}) 8. Bb2 a6 ({An original idea but not a good one. The
normal} 8... Qc7 {and 9...e4 would be better - Kotov.}) 9. d4 Rb8 ({If} 9... b5
10. Nd2 $1 Qc7 11. cxb5 axb5 12. Nxb5 $1) 10. Nd2 c5 ({The begining of a plan
that eventually proves faulty. If Black wanted complications, then} 10... e5
11. dxe5 Nxe5 $5 {was the safest way to obtain them.}) 11. dxc5 Nxc5 (11...
dxc5 {would have been better. If} 12. Qc2 e6 {and Black can set up a defence -
Kotov.}) 12. b4 Ne6 13. Nb3 ({[Black had been] hoping for} 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14.
Bxg7 Ne3 {with interesting possibilities.}) 13... Ng4 $2 {Committing the
elementary error of starting operations before completing my development.} 14.
e3 Ne5 15. Qe2 b5 $5 {Continuing my reckless way; there was still some chance
of developing my remaining pieces.} 16. cxb5 axb5 17. Na5 $1 ({And not} 17.
Nxb5 Ba6 18. a4 Qd7 19. N3d4 $1 Nc7 $1 20. f4 Nxb5 21. fxe5 Nxd4 22. Qxa6 Nc2 {
giving Black good counterplay.}) 17... Bd7 {The only move but not quite
adequate.} 18. f4 Nc4 19. Nxc4 bxc4 20. Qxc4 {A risky move, as Kotov admits
that he did not see my reply.} Qc8 $1 {This is the idea of Black's combination.
After exchanging the queens, he would have excellent play - Kotov.} 21. Qd3 {
Exchanging would favour Black.} Nc5 ({As} 21... Rxb4 $2 {fails to} 22. Nd5 {
, I am forced to fish in troubled waters.}) 22. Qd1 ({If now} 22. bxc5 Rxb2 23.
Nd5 Bb5 $1 ({After} 23... Qxc5 24. Nxe7+ Kh8 {, Black would have two good
bishops and a fine game - Kotov}) 24. Nxe7+ Kh8 25. Nxc8 Bxd3 26. Rfd1 Rxc8 27.
Rxd3 Rxg2+ $1 28. Kxg2 Bxa1 {and Black wins.}) ({On the other hand, if the
queen goes to the second rank - e.g.} 22. Qd2 {- Black can play} Rxb4 23. Nd5
Rxb2 $1 {winning a piece}) ({So} 22. Qd1 {is the only move, when} Bg4 23. Bf3
Bxf3 24. Rxf3 Rxb4 $1 25. Nd5 Rb7 {guards the e-pawn; the point of my play.
But now Kotov reveals what a great tactician he is by pinpointing the serious
flaw in my combination.}) 22... Bg4 (22... Na4 23. Nxa4 Bxa4 ({nor in this line
} 23... Rxb4 24. Bxg7 Kxg7 25. Nb2 $1 Rxb2 26. Qd4+) 24. Qxa4 Bxb2 25. Rab1 Qc3
26. b5 $1 {is not good enough for Black.}) 23. Nd5 $3 Bxb2 {The winning move.
It is interesting to note that White's combination would be impossible if
Black's h-pawn were on h6. It is on such small things that tactics are based!
Black could now resign as the following variations show:} (23... Re8 24. Qd2 $1
{and Black has no compensation for his pawn.}) (23... Bxd1 24. Nxe7+ Kh8 25.
Bxg7+ Kxg7 26. Nxc8 Rxb4 27. Rfxd1 Rxc8 28. Rxd6 {, etc.}) (23... Qe6 24. Qd2
Ne4 25. Bxe4 Bxb2 26. Qxb2 Qxe4 27. Qd4 $1 Qxd4 28. exd4 {and Black has no
move.}) 24. Nxe7+ Kg7 25. Qd2 Bxa1 (25... Ne4 26. Qxb2+ Qc3 27. Qxc3+ Nxc3 $18
28. Nc6 {, etc}) (25... Qe6 26. Qxb2+ f6 27. Nd5) 26. Nxc8 Bf6 27. Nxd6 Na4 28.
Ne4 Rfd8 29. Qc2 Rxb4 30. Nxf6 Kxf6 31. a3 Rb2 32. Qxa4 Rdd2 33. Qa8 Bd1 34.
Qh8+ {Source: CHESS/28/424-425 January 1963, p112; The Times, 7 January 1963} (
34. Qh8+ {Among other things, White can play} Ke7 35. Qxb2 Rxb2 36. Rxd1 {
. An enjoyable game from anybody's point of view but mine.}) 1-0

